  "query_preview": "..\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\nWith an upper low over south-central Canada, the upper flow over\nnortheast Montana this afternoon was northwest. This is making \nfor another windy day across the area with wind gusts around 40 \nmph.\n\nDaytime heating and sufficient humidity allowed for cumulus clouds\nto develop but showers have been isolated with the dewpoints\nmostly in the 40s (noted that the showers were more numerous over\nsouthern Sask and western ND with dewpoints in the 50s).\n\nActivity will diminish by evening with loss of daytime heating but\na disturbance will keep showers going across central Montana and \ninto the western zones tonight. A Lake Wind Advisory expiring at \n9 pm looks good.\n\nWinds pick up again Friday with a Lake Wind Advisory likely needed\nfor the afternoon. Cumulus will build again and produce isolated \nshowers.\n\nSaturday should feature the coolest day with highs in the 60s and\nlows the following night mainly in the 40s, but some 30s are\npossible in some of the cooler valleys.\n\nThings begin to warm a bit Sunday through Tuesday but a couple\ndisturbances look to drop southeast in the region. This will bring\nthe chance of showers and isolated thunderstorms.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nConfidence then remains above average for cool and unsettled \nweather to continue for next several days given the NW flow\ncontinuing. Confidence in exact timing, placement, and amount of \nprecipitation remains low to moderate given the mesoscale detail \nstill to be worked out a larger time scales.\n\nTherefore, used CAMS and NBM for shower chances through Friday.\nUsed the default NBM for the remainder of the forecast. TFJ",
